    "body": "@masayuki-nakano Thank you for taking a look.\nOne concrete cause is generic `keydown` shortcut handling such as Enter-to-submit:\n\n```js\ninput.addEventListener(\"keydown\", event => {\n  if (event.key === \"Enter\") submit();\n});\n```\n\nThere are still real sites and web applications, including large ones, that implement this kind of logic without checking `event.isComposing`.\n\nFor IME users, Enter may be used to confirm the current conversion candidate rather than to submit the form. If the application treats that Enter key as an ordinary application command, the text can be submitted accidentally while the user is still composing.\n\nThis is the kind of issue described in [1] and [2]. In particular, those examples show how ordinary application shortcuts such as Enter-to-submit can conflict with IME composition.\n\nSo even if browsers expose `isComposing === true` correctly for `keydown`, the current model still relies on every web application author knowing about IME composition and remembering to special-case it. This is easy to miss for authors who do not regularly use an IME themselves.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
